eet these  characters in their normal lives before  their lives are changed forever on  Fantasy Island but there is this  incredible elegance to meeting them as  the plane arrives and as the door opens  and it was such a part of the rhythm of  the show that I really wanted to embrace  it with the film in my car  writers Chris Roach and Jill Jacobs  ultimately agreed and and interestingly  enough before we even wrote the movie  and when you see the movie there's  obviously a teaser I'm talking to the  fans at home there's a teaser but  originally didn't begin with that teaser  originally the movie began on the beach  with mr. works assistant Julia and the  first line of the movie was the plane  and I always felt that that was really  important that we have that as kind of  an iconic moment very early in the film  so it is the first line of dialogue you  hear after the title I'm just curious  because the three of you have worked  together often like what is your  favorite horror movie or what's the type  of horror movie you gravitate towards  and what about the two of them because  you tap into so many different spheres  in this movie well this is a big jean  room a summary but there's time travel  there's sci-fi there's intense drama  there's actually quite a few laughs I  believe because we're basically doing  four different movies but my favorite  kind of horror is character based horror  right it's what Blum House does so well  it's when you have characters who are on  a journey there who are gonna test it  and changed by their circumstances when  you get a chance to connect with them  that's the best kind of horror because  it's not just about gore it's about  feeling something real wholeheartedly  agree with that so for fantasies here  why specifically the number four and was  there any particular fantasy you found  toughest to weave into the the overall  narrative at play well there are four  because I thought a lot about the  structure of the show you know the show  always had two fantasies over two hours  I'm sorry over one hour so we're doing  it two hour movie so four seemed like  the right number as far as the specific  fantasies you know we were just we just  started off by looking at all the  episodes we basically came up with log  lines for the different episodes we  don't watch them all again but we looked  at them all all the the plot  descriptions and we remembered our  favorites and we wrote them down and  then we started riffing and trying to  come up with fantasies that if we were  just redoing the show fantasies we would  want to see in an episode of the show  and then we started to push those  through the filter of the larger idea I  brought to the film about how they might  be all related was there any one that  was the most difficult to shoot  especially working on Fiji which sounds  like a dream come true to me but I  imagine there are some serious  production challenges that come with  that they were all difficult to shoot  none of them were easy  you know part of making a plumb house  movies that you have a very limited  budget we made this movie for 7.2  million dollars Wow not a lot of money  you know there are no trailers for the  cast no I didn't have a techno crane one  day on this movie I mean I had a techno  crane on my first movie which is a  million dollar movie didn't have any  sophisticated filmmaking equipment we  basically had the gear that we barged  over for news from New Zealand if  anything broke it was broken you know  that we just just had to figure it out  that is that's something else that that  figure on a movie that feels like it  could be for movies is pretty wildly  impressive there yes it was it was not  easy how was a cruise ship living for  you were you okay on it yes sir the  first two weeks we'll a little backstory  we couldn't find the house the the main  island location and I looked everywhere  around the more populated parts of Fiji  and we just couldn't find this colonial  looking building because by and large  you're sort of a Polynesian aesthetic in  Fiji eventually we found this building  but it was in a very remote part of Fiji  and I said to the line producer that we  got a shoot here this has got to be it  and we're coming down the wire we're  listening like maybe six weeks away from  starting to shoot and I had had a plan  but it wasn't a great plan and I said  this is a much better plan she was like  well they don't have the ability to  support us there there's no there's no  there's no hotels that the airplanes  that can land they're only like 15  Cedars we can't get the equipment there  in time and we can't do it I mean the  only way we could possibly do it is if  we bought out a cruise ship and I said  well why don't we buy out a cruise ship  so we bought out a cruise ship for the  first two weeks of photography we put  the cast the crew on it we lashed all  the gear the deck we had the kick-off  party on a Saturday night we all went to  bed in our cabins we sailed 17 hours so  the other side of Fiji we woke up we  start shooting this on the one hand this  sounds like a dream come true for an  on-location shoot it also sounds like  the start of a horror movie within a  horror movie  yeah it definitely had potential to be  problematic and I think by the end of  the two weeks everyone was ready to get  off the boat yeah the hotel  but fortunately really we just had such  an amazing  cast such a fantastic crew everyone  immediately bonded I think because we  were on the boat and we just became a  family your cast is really something  else in this when you're working with an  ensemble this big and characters that  wind up being so pivotal to each other  do you find yourself saying like this is  the character I have to cast first and  then build the in Samba around them  well mr. Rourke yes obviously had to be  cast first so grateful that Michael Pena  responded to the script they wanted to  do it I actually flew to Australia to  take him out to dinner and ask him to do  the movie and he's such a great guy  gracious guy at dinner he was like oh no  I'm already in like I told my reps I  wanted to do it you didn't have to fly  to Australia to take me out to dinner he  was there shooting Dora the Explorer so  that that was obviously the most pivotal  role that needed to be cast and then the  rest just sort of fell into place you  know some would cast very quickly Lucy  obviously I was cast very quickly Austin  Stowell is honestly the first actor that  read any words that I had written for  the movie and but we were trying to go a  different way with Patrick even though I  knew he was right and ultimately I was  just like it's Austin I mean we can't  keep trying to force this to be  something it's not Austin is the guy  yeah he got the part when the the second  he read we were just it took us a little  while to realize that you know he is it  other actors sort of emerge over the  course of the process of casting but  ultimately we got the perfect actor for  every part because one of the things I  really believe is I always cast the  actor not the role which means I'm  looking for someone interesting who  brings something from their own life to  the to them to the movie to the story  and adds all these dimensions that I  could have never fathom and as soon as I  find that I know that that's the person  I dig that approach quite a bit as an  actor's director jumping from scenarios  that are so different from each other do  you find yourself I mean maybe you  scheduled it in a way where you can  knock out one fantasy at a time but they  all they all fall in like different  subgenres do you find it challenging to  adjust from one to the other and then  back again no because there was a sort  of overriding theme of emotion and  living and regret and we tried to make  sure it permeated  every story so even though I was  attacking different genres almost within  each fantasy I wanted this to I 